{
  "reports": [
    {
      "title": "Automated Loop Restoration into Unisolated Fault at Synthetic Feeder 7",
      "core_units": [
        {
          "text": "At 02:16:40, the restoration controller trusted the cached open position of Synthetic LS-7B instead of reading the closed auxiliary contact.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The Synthetic LS-7B status was stuck at 21:36:52 because the manual close report from the field never reached the SCADA system.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The controller's freshness filter was disabled, so the five-hour-old switch state remained acceptable during the storm restoration profile.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The lockout relay 86-S7 did not cause the later tie closure; it remained latched after the initial 52-S7 trip until manually reset.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:15:00, a storm-driven tree limb contacted a conductor on Synthetic Main Feeder 7, producing a 3.2 kA ground fault.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:15:12, the feeder relay at Synthetic Substation 07 issued a trip signal to circuit breaker 52-S7 after 0.38 s of overcurrent.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:15:14, lockout relay 86-S7 operated and prevented circuit breaker 52-S7 from closing back automatically.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:16:44, the loop restoration controller detected three-phase loss of voltage on the load side of Synthetic LS-7B and started restoration.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:16:51, the controller closed tie switch TS-7/12 because all displayed preconditions, including a false open LS-7B, were satisfied.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            0,
            1,
            2,
            7
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:16:53, measured current on Synthetic Tie Feeder 12 rose to 4.6 kA as the tie connected to the faulted segment behind LS-7B.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:16:56, circuit breaker 52-T12 tripped on its instantaneous element and started a 15-second reclosing interval while TS-7/12 remained closed.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:17:29, circuit breaker 52-T12 made its third close into the fault, tripped, and lockout relay 86-T12 operated.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            10
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:17:29, lockout relay 86-T12 latched and kept Synthetic Tie Feeder 12 de-energized until an operator reset it at 03:12.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            11
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Between 02:16:51 and 02:17:32, the loop controller processed 176 switching commands into its event buffer, saturating the queue.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            8,
            11
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Energy not supplied on Synthetic Tie Feeder 12 was 2.11 MWh, calculated from a metered pre-fault load of 2.3 MW over 55 minutes.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 02:19:32, Synthetic Main Feeder 7 upstream of LS-7B remained de-energized because breaker 52-S7 and lockout relay 86-S7 were still open.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:12, an operator manually reset lockout relay 86-T12 and placed tie switch TS-7/12 in local hold mode.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:39, a field crew opened Synthetic LS-7B by local controls, physically isolating the faulted section from the alternate source.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            16
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 04:00, engineering connected the independent auxiliary contact LS-7B-PROX into the restoration logic and verified that it reported real-time state.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            17
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 04:18, operator changed controller settings to expire every switch-state cache after 120 seconds during storm restoration.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            18
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distractor_seeds": [
        "Automatic loop restoration logic normally uses a dead-bus check on the load side before closing a tie switch.",
        "ANSI 52 identifies a circuit breaker, and ANSI 86 identifies a lockout relay used in the breaker control diagram.",
        "Field tests of wireless mesh communication are often scheduled before peak storm seasons.",
        "A feeder capacitor bank controller uses voltage and current inputs to regulate power factor at a distribution substation.",
        "Synchronized phasor measurements can detect the phase angle difference across a normally open distribution tie.",
        "A triac has two main terminals, MT1 and MT2, plus a gate used to initiate conduction each half-cycle.",
        "Conductor ampacity ratings are reduced when ambient temperature exceeds the expected summer design value.",
        "An automatic transfer scheme checks both source-side and load-side voltages through separate transducers.",
        "Circuit breaker maintenance intervals are commonly based on the number of interrupted fault currents.",
        "Storm damage reports from customer calls are integrated into outage management systems for dispatching crews.",
        "Remote switching orders specify whether a controller is in local or remote mode before an operator acts.",
        "Protective relay settings should be verified after any feeder reconfiguration changes the source impedance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Solid-State Tie Switch Latch Causes Parallel Restoration Loop at Synthetic North Feeder 22",
      "core_units": [
        {
          "text": "At 03:14:10, a wind-blown branch struck a conductor on Synthetic North Feeder 22, causing a 4.1 kA phase-to-ground fault.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:14:23, phase-overcurrent relay protection sent a trip signal to circuit breaker 52-N22, opening Synthetic North Feeder 22.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:14:25, lockout relay 86-N22 latched and prevented circuit breaker 52-N22 from automatic reclosure.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:15:27, after Synthetic LS-22F opened the faulted segment, the automation controller initialized the solid-state tie closure sequence.",
          "role": "trigger_event",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            2
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A damaged gate-driver component left 4.2 V on the gate terminal for 12 ms, delaying block recovery of the phase-A triac.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:18:40, the phase-A triac in Synthetic TS-22/23 failed to block at a current zero, so MT1-to-MT2 conduction continued after gate removal.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The controller's open indication came from gate-supply voltage zero, not from the independent 22/23-VM voltage monitor, which still indicated conduction.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Phase-B and phase-C triacs in Synthetic TS-22/23 blocked normally after their gate pulses ceased, so the fault was confined to phase A.",
          "role": "root_cause",
          "causal_predecessors": []
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:15:42, the loop controller closed Synthetic TS-22/23, re-energizing the unfaulted part of Synthetic North Feeder 22 from Synthetic South Feeder 23.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:18:42, the controller considered Synthetic TS-22/23 open after stopping its gate pulses, but phase-A current remained at 310 A.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            5,
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:19:07, the operator closed circuit breaker 52-N22, paralleling Synthetic North and South feeders through the still-conducting phase-A triac.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:19:09, circuit breaker 52-S23 tripped on directional overcurrent because measured current rose from 310 A to 2.0 kA within 40 ms.",
          "role": "propagation_or_response",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            10
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:19:11, lockout relay 86-S23 latched, interrupting Synthetic South Feeder 23 and all restored Synthetic North Feeder 22 load.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            11
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:20:40, the independent voltage monitor 22/23-VM read 0.4 V between MT1 and MT2, disproving the controller's open-state indication for phase-A.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            5,
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Energy unserved equaled 3.67 MWh, computed from pre-fault loads of 1.8 MW and 1.3 MW over 71 minutes.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:21:15, an independent phase-rotation monitor on Synthetic North Feeder 22 recorded zero positive-sequence voltage, confirming no other source had restored it.",
          "role": "impact",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 03:58, an operator de-energized the triac gate drive and manually verified that MT1-to-MT2 voltage reached 12.0 kV, proving forward blocking.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 04:12, a field crew replaced the defective triac gate-driver board and the damaged MT1-to-MT2 voltage monitor on Synthetic TS-22/23.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 04:31, engineering updated the controller logic to require independent voltage monitor 22/23-VM to read below 5 V before allowing a feeder breaker close.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            6,
            17
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "At 05:20, technicians ran a controlled restoration test and verified all triac modules blocked before each circuit breaker closure.",
          "role": "mitigation",
          "causal_predecessors": [
            17,
            18
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distractor_seeds": [
        "In a triac, the gate terminal is referenced to MT1 while load current flows from MT2 through the semiconductor material.",
        "ANSI 86 lockout relays are reset manually by an operator after protective lockout to prevent automatic reclosure.",
        "Distribution control centers use sequence-of-events logging to reconstruct relay timing after a disturbance.",
        "Power system transient simulations often model a solid-state switch as a variable resistance that changes with gate triggering.",
        "A normally open tie switch is not necessarily closed during a storm unless load-side voltage loss is confirmed.",
        "Load estimation uses measured feeder head energy divided by elapsed time and adjusted for losses.",
        "High-speed transfer switches can limit voltage sags to just a few milliseconds for sensitive industrial loads.",
        "The phase angle difference across an open tie is caused by load flow and source voltage variations, not just generator angles.",
        "Feeder fault current includes an asymmetrical DC offset that decays with the system X/R ratio.",
        "SCADA controllers store recent state changes in nonvolatile memory to survive communication restarts.",
        "A complete feeder model includes cable lengths, conductor sizes, connected load taps, and transformer impedances.",
        "Capacitor banks may be switched by thyristor valves with forced commutation for fast dynamic response."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
